## Final Judgement
The fact-check evidence clearly demonstrates that Robin Vos, the Republican Speaker of the Wisconsin State Assembly, did not lose his own seat (District 63) in the 2018 election; he won re-election with 61% of the vote. The sources consistently show he was the incumbent who held and successfully defended his seat. There is no evidence provided about any other specific Republican-held seat being lost under his leadership in that election.

The key ambiguity in the claim lies in its phrasing: whether it refers to Vos personally losing his seat, or to him, as Speaker, overseeing the loss of a Republican-held seat in the broader chamber. The evidence conclusively refutes the first interpretation. For the second interpretation, the record does not contain information on the overall partisan seat changes in the 2018 Wisconsin Assembly elections, which would be necessary to verify if a net loss of Republican seats occurred for the first time under his speakership.

Therefore, based solely on the recorded evidence, the claim as it is most literally interpreted—that Robin Vos himself lost an election—is refuted. However, due to the potential for a different interpretation about broader chamber results, for which there is no supporting or refuting evidence in the record, the most precise decision is:

`not enough information`

### Verdict: NEI

### Justification
The fact-check refutes the claim that Robin Vos lost his own seat, as evidence shows he was the incumbent Republican in [Wisconsin State Assembly District 63](https://madison.com/robin-vos-timeline/article_ce58f640-24d2-5fdb-9c8f-ce3a3930ba88.html) and won re-election in 2018 with 61% of the vote. While the record confirms he did not personally lose, it does not contain information on whether other Republican-held seats were lost under his speakership, leading to a verdict of `not enough information` due to the claim's potential ambiguity.